  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.5" extension="2015-08-01"/><templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.5.1" extension="2015-08-01"/>
    <code code="11450-4" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Problem List"/>
    <title>Problems</title>
    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>New fatigue and slowly increasing weakness for about 18 months, in the left leg and both arms as well as the right leg weakened by polio at seven, with aching muscle pain after work and cold intolerance of the right leg; cause to be established</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>M62.81</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting concern at the index visit, superseded by the diagnosis of post-polio syndrome at the second visit; the paralytic polio of 1902 is documented (D).</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Post-polio syndrome (modified March of Dimes criteria met): new weakness of the left quadriceps and both shoulder girdles and increased weakness of the right leg, with fatigue, muscle pain, and cold intolerance, more than 50 years after paralytic polio with partial recovery and decades of stable function; nerve conduction normal; EMG chronic reinnervation in both legs and the right deltoid; other causes excluded</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>G14</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis; the childhood polio is documented (D), and her biographer&apos;s account of post-polio symptoms later in life is a retrospective interpretation (R). No neurological finding from her lifetime is used.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Heartburn and regurgitation after meals and at night for three months, three to four times a week; no difficulty swallowing, weight loss, vomiting, bleeding, or anemia</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R12</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting symptom at the index visit, superseded by the endoscopic diagnosis at the fourth visit.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-6">
    <td>Osteoporosis, worst at the right hip on the polio-weakened side (right femoral neck T-score -2.7; left -1.6; lumbar spine -1.8), with vitamin D insufficiency; no vertebral fracture on assessment; 10-year FRAX risk about 16 percent major osteoporotic and 4.5 percent hip</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>M81.8</td><td>S — Synthetic bone density result; disuse of a polio-affected limb lowers bone density on that side.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-7">
    <td>Two falls in the past year without injury, from catching the right foot</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>Z91.81</td><td>S — Synthetic fall history at presentation.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-8">
    <td>Surgery for severe stomach ulcers in her early fifties; no ulcer symptoms since; avoids nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>Z87.11</td><td>S — Synthetic modern history entry linked to the documented ulcers and surgery of the late 1940s (D); the type of operation is not known.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-9">
    <td>Acute-phase reaction two days after the first zoledronic acid infusion: fever to 38.1 °C, muscle and joint aching, and headache, without signs of infection; calcium normal; resolved in three days with acetaminophen and fluids</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>T50.995A</td><td>S — Synthetic expected adverse effect of a first intravenous bisphosphonate dose.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

