    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute-phase reaction after the first zoledronic acid infusion (T50.995A)</content>. Fever, muscle and joint aching, and headache starting within a day of a first intravenous bisphosphonate dose, with no focal signs of infection, normal white cell count, and normal calcium and creatinine, is the expected acute-phase reaction. It usually settles within three days and is much less likely with later infusions. It is not a worsening of post-polio syndrome; her strength is unchanged. [S]<list><item>Acetaminophen 1000 mg up to three times a day for three days, no more than 3000 mg a day; no ibuprofen or naproxen after her ulcer surgery.</item><item>Fluids and rest; resume her exercise program when the fever settles.</item><item>Call if fever lasts beyond three days, rises above 39 °C, or if eye pain, cramps, or tingling develop.</item><item>For next year&apos;s infusion, acetaminophen before and for two days after to lessen the reaction.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Post-polio syndrome (G14)</content>. Aching from the infusion reaction, not new weakness. She was told plainly that a field day next week is reasonable if the fever has gone, with her usual pacing. [S]<list><item>Keep next week&apos;s field day with rest breaks; skip the following exercise session if still tired.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: EFNS guideline on diagnosis and management of post-polio syndrome. Report of an EFNS task force. Eur J Neurol 2006;13(8):795-801 (2006); RECLAST (zoledronic acid) injection: prescribing information (acute phase reaction, calcium and kidney checks, osteonecrosis of the jaw) (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Telephone check in two days. Endoscopy as booked next week; she continues to use the antacid as needed until then. Next scheduled visit 1957-05-18: endoscopy results and reflux treatment; post-polio syndrome follow-up. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
